THOUGHT: The patient is an 8-year-old boy with lifelong (onset at 6 months), generalized dry, scaly, coarse skin that worsens in winter and improves slightly in summer — consistent with a chronic, inherited keratinization disorder. Key clinical features include:  
- Generalized fine scaling with sparing of flexural creases (i.e., neck, antecubital, popliteal fossae) — this spares the typical distribution of atopic dermatitis or contact dermatitis;  
- Follicular hyperkeratosis (papular elevations at hair follicle bases) — hallmark of ichthyosis vulgaris;  
- Hyperlinear palms and soles with darkening of palmar/plantar creases (hyperlinearity + accentuated crease pigmentation) — highly characteristic of ichthyosis vulgaris;  
- Absence of pruritus, erythema, or inflammation — argues against inflammatory dermatoses (e.g., atopic dermatitis, psoriasis);  
- Normal thyroid function and CBC — rules out acquired causes like hypothyroidism or nutritional deficiencies;  
- Negative allergy testing — excludes allergic contact or atopic triggers;  
- Skin biopsy shows compact hyperkeratosis and diminished/absent granular layer — the classic histopathologic triad of ichthyosis vulgaris (hyperkeratosis, reduced granular layer, no acanthosis or spongiosis).  
The autosomal semi-dominant inheritance pattern is often seen, but absence of family history does not exclude it (de novo mutations or variable expressivity may occur). While other ichthyoses (e.g., X-linked recessive ichthyosis, lamellar ichthyosis) present in infancy, they typically show more severe scaling (plate-like or collodion membrane), involve flexures, and have distinct histopathology (e.g., cholesterol clefts in X-linked, absent lamellar granules in lamellar). This case lacks those features. The age of onset, seasonal variation, follicular keratosis, palmar hyperlinearity, and biopsy findings are all highly specific for ichthyosis vulgaris.

DIAGNOSIS: Ichthyosis vulgaris

